## 1. Introduction
Although advances in technologies have enhanced our quality of life, they have exacerbated environmental including electromagnetic pollution. The significant and ever-increasing demand for wireless communication technologies, including the Internet of Things, portable electronics, and the emergence of beyond 5G cellular technologies, led to crowding the scarce and finite spectrum frequency bands. While time, spatial and frequency multiplexing provide means for signal diversity, limitations that restrict the quality of transmission remain. Lightweight, ductile, insulating, and frequency tunable electromagnetic absorbing materials can potentially play a significant role in filtering and fading unwanted signals. Among the electromagnetic wave responsive materials, absorbing materials prepared on the basis of magnetic ferrite [1], magnetic metals [2], semiconductor materials (such as silicon carbide [3], zinc oxide [4], etc.), and conducting materials (such as graphene [5], carbon nanotubes [6], etc.) have been widely investigated. In considering single component materials, the magnetic and dielectric properties play an essential role in the loss of electromagnetic waves. For applications of electromagnetic absorption, in semiconducting materials, electron transport suffers from less degrees of freedom due to the existence of the bandgap. Conversely, in conducting materials, the surface effects, including eddy currents under high-frequency incident electromagnetic waves, prevent most electromagnetic waves from entering the material, resulting in poor impedance matching [3]. Therefore, when considering the design of electromagnetic wave absorbing materials while accounting for ways to maximize the loss of incident electromagnetic waves, attention should also be paid to the material composition and structure design to achieve excellent impedance matching.
Over the past years, metal organic frameworks (MOFs) have been widely used in photocatalysis, batteries, electrocatalysis and other fields [7,8,9,10]. Their unique structure and simple preparation protocols have also greatly enriched the selection ranges of electromagnetic wave absorbing materials [11,12,13]. In MOFs, different combinations of metal ions and organic ligands can construct different spatial and topological structures [14,15]. By adjusting the organic ligands and metal ions, the morphology and microscopic properties can be changed, and they can be used as precursors, and subsequent treatment can be carried out to obtain carbon and metal derivatives. Che et al. used Ni and Co as metal ions and added two different ligands to prepare bimetallic MOFs derivatives. By adjusting the ratio of the two metal ions to adjust the electromagnetic parameters, off-axis electron holographic lenses were used to observe space charge accumulation and potential difference at the heterojunction interface [16]. However, it is noteworthy that although the derivatives obtained by pyrolysis of MOFs can meet most of the electromagnetic wave absorption requirements, the higher carbon contents due to presence of graphite can potentially result in higher conductivity and hinder the electromagnetic wave from entering free space. Therefore, by constructing a heterojunction structure of MOFs derivatives, while providing a large number of heterojunction interfaces, the impedance matching of the absorbing material can be better adjusted, and its performance can be improved.
At the same time, LDH is an emerging class of nanomaterials, composed of positively charged layered materials and negatively charged intercalation anions, which has been used in lithium battery materials, photocatalysis and other fields [17,18,19]. Interestingly, there are some subtle conversion relationships between the two materials, LDH and MOFs. Wang et al. used liquid-phase transmission electron microscopy to observe the transition from ZIF-8 to LDH. During the transition progress, ZIF-8 nanoparticles were converted to hollow LDH nanocages by the growth of LDH nanosheets [20]. Zhao et al prepared the ZIF 67 polyhedron with dimethylimidazole as the ligand, explored the growth mode of ZIF 67 and Co LDHs on the carbon fiber surface under different synthesis conditions, and proved that different synthesis environments have a greater influence on the composition of the product [21]. Wu et al. obtained wafer-like MOFs/LDH/MOFs by preparing sheet-like CoAl LDH and growing ZIF 67 in situ on its surface. It is believed that the existence of Co ions on the twodimensional surface of LDHs can serve as the initial nucleation site of ZIF 67, which provides the possibility for further growth of topological structures [22]. It can be seen from the existing body of literature that there is a certain degree of chemical compatibility between MOFs and LDH. Additionally, as an electromagnetic wave absorbing material, LDH and its derivative have a variety of electromagnetic wave loss mechanisms [23,24,25,26,27]. However, the current synthesis method of LDH is limited to using the corresponding metal salt to synthesize in an alkaline environment. Few papers report the influence of microstructure and composition of LDH and their derivatives on electromagnetic wave absorption performance [23,53]. Interestingly, room temperature liquid metal can improve the green and efficient reaction platform for large-scale preparation of 2D $\mathsf{A l}(\mathsf{O H})_{3}$ . The flexible liquid metal and surface provide a mild reaction environment for the growth of $\mathsf{A l}(\mathsf{O H})_{3}$ . The unique crystal structure and microscopic morphology of two-dimensional $\mathsf{A l}(\mathsf{O H})_{3}$ suggest that it can integrate Al-based LDH on a large scale.
Herein, we use room temperature liquid metal alloy-driven unique 2D hydroxide morphologies to synthesize MOF (ZIF 67) incorporated layered double oxides through stirring, the aging process, and annealing to obtain the electromagnetic wave absorbing CoAl-LDO@Co-C nano-arrays, in which the nano-arrays refer to regular geometrical shapes including surface cubic and honeycomb structures. Under the combined effect of magnetic loss and dielectric loss, CoAl-LDO@Co-C-3 exhibits excellent electromagnetic wave absorption performance and highly effective absorption bandwidth $2.6\mathrm{mm}$ and $8.48\:{\mathrm{GHz}}$ respectively). This work presents a pioneering synthesis method of Al-based nano morphologies which can be incorporated into many other research areas, including electrochemical energy storage, lithium-ion batteries, supercapacitors and photocatalysis.
## 2. Experiment
## 2.1. Materials
Cobalt nitrate hexahydrate $({\mathsf{C o}}({\mathsf{N O}}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6{\mathsf{H}}_{2}{\mathsf{O}},$ $99\%$ ), dimethylimidazole (2-MI), methanol (MeOH), ethanol $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{CH}_{2}0\mathrm{H};$ , aluminum
powder $(99.9\%)$ , gallium $(99.99\%)$ and indium $(99.99\%)$ were purchased from Nanjing Jingge Chem. Co. China.
## 2.2. Preparation of two-dimensional $A l(O H)_{3}$
EGaIn was prepared in the lab using previously reported methods [28]. Gallium and indium were added into a glass beaker with a weight ratio of 3:1 and heated above the melting point of indium $(157^{\circ}\mathsf C)$ while stirred with a magnetic stirrer. Al powder was then added to the EGaIn inside a nitrogen-purged glovebox. 4 mmol of Al powder was mixed with $_{5\mathrm{g}}$ of the liquid metal alloy, and ground with a mortar and pestle for $30\mathrm{min}$ . Al was mixed into the liquid metal until a significant change in the gloss of the liquid metal surface was observed. The GaInAl liquid metal alloy was removed from the glovebox, and $60\mathrm{ml}$ of deionized water was immediately added on top of the alloy. After the mixture was allowed to react for $^{2\mathrm{h}}$ , in which the reaction was completed, a white hydrogel was obtained.
## 2.3. Preparation of CoAl-LDH
The prepared $\mathsf{A l}(\mathsf{O H})_{3}$ was added to $30\mathrm{ml}$ of deionized water, the mixture was ultrasonically dispersed for $10\mathrm{min}$ , and then $4\mathrm{mmol}$ of cobalt nitrate and $4\mathrm{mmol}$ of dimethylimidazole (Al: ${\mathsf{C o}}{:}2{\mathrm{-}}{\mathrm{MI}}=1{:}1{:}1.$ ) was added to the mixture, and stirred at room temperature for $^{3\mathrm{h}}$ . It could be observed that the liquid gradually turned from purple to brown, washed with pure ethanol, centrifuged at $10,000~\mathrm{RCF}$ , and then placed in an oven at $60^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ to dry overnight.
## 2.4. Preparation of CoAl-LDH@ZIF 67
Firstly, the prepared CoAl-LDH was added into $20\mathrm{ml}$ of MeOH, denoted as solution A. Also, $20\mathrm{ml}$ MeOH contained 4 mmol of cobalt nitrate and $4\mathrm{mmol}$ dimethylimidazole ${\mathrm{'}}{\mathrm{Co}};2{\mathrm{-}}{\mathrm{MI}}=1{:}1$ , denoted as solution B), were quickly added to solution A at room temperature and stirred for $^{3\mathrm{h}}$ , which was then allowed to stand for $^{3\mathrm{h}}$ . Ultimately, the product is washed with pure ethanol, centrifuged at 10,000 RCF, and placed in an oven at $60^{\circ}C$ overnight. The product was named CoAl-LDH $@$ ZIF-X ( $\mathrm{\Delta}X=1,2$ , and 3), in which we refer to ZIF 67 simply as ZIF omitting 67, following letter X indicating the weight proportion of CoAl-LDH during synthesis. Three different weight contents of the CoAl-LDH precursor (0.06, 0.12 and $0.24~\mathrm{g}\dot{}\$ in solution A were prepared. The obtained products were recorded as CoAl-LDH@ZIF-3, CoAl-LDH@ZIF-2 and CoAlLDH $@$ ZIF-1.
## 2.5. Preparation of $A l(O H)_{3}@Z I F$ 67
Referring to the preparation of the CoAl-LDH (0.12 g) in the previous section. $\mathsf{A l}(\mathsf{O H})_{3}$ directly obtained from liquid metals was used without incorporating Co ions to obtain $\mathsf{A l}(0\mathrm{H})_{3}@\mathsf{Z I F}$ 67.
## 2.6. Preparation of CoAl-LDO@Co-C
CoAl-LDH, $\mathsf{A l}(0\mathsf{H})_{3}@\mathsf{Z I F}$ 67, CoAl-LDH@ZIF-1, CoAl-LDH@ZIF-2, and CoAl-LDH $@$ ZIF-3 were placed into a tube furnace and annealed at $800^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for two hours under nitrogen environment. The products obtained were denoted as CoAl-LDO, $\mathsf{A l}_{2}\mathsf{O}_{3}@\mathsf{C o}-\mathsf{C}$ , CoAl-LDO@CoC-1, CoAl-LDO@Co-C-2, and CoAl-LDO@Co-C-3, respectively.
## 2.7. Characterizations
The crystal structure of the sample was characterized using Bruker D8 Advance X-ray diffraction (XRD). The degree of graphitization and the vibration modes of chemical bonds in the sample were obtained using Raman spectroscopy (Raman, WItec, Alpha 300R). The elemental oxidation state of the samples was confirmed by X-ray photoelectron spectrometry (XPS, Thermo Scientific, ESCALAB 250XI) with monochromatic Al $\mathsf{K}\mathsf{\ensuremath{\upalpha}}$ radiation. PerkinElmer 2000 spectrometer (USA) was used to analyze the FTIR spectrum, in order to realize the chemical bond and composition of the prepared product. The surface characterization of the samples was tested by surface area and porosity analyzer (BET ASAP 2020 PLUS). The morphologies and microstructure of the samples were characterized using Transmission Electron Microscopy (TEM, JEOL JEM 2100), and Scanning Electron Microscopy (SEM, HITACHI) which was equipped with energy-dispersive X-ray spectroscopy (EDX) to detect the element distribution. The magnetic properties of the samples were tested using a Vibrating Sample Magnetometer (VSM, Quantum Design EZ9).
The electromagnetic parameters, complex permittivity $\mathrm{\{\varepsilon_{c_{r}}=\varepsilon_{c_{\rho}^{\prime}-}}}$ je‘‘) and permeability $(\upmu_{\mathrm{r}}=\upmu^{\prime}-\up j\upmu^{\prime\prime})$ , of the samples in the frequency range of $2{-}18\operatorname{GHz}$ were measured using a vector network analyzer (VNA, CETE Ceyear 3672B). All the samples were uniformly dispersed with paraffin wax with a filler loading of 30 wt $\%$ and pressed into a circular shape with an outer diameter of $7.0\mathrm{mm}$ and an inner diameter of $3.04\mathrm{mm}$ .
## 3. Results and discussion
Fig. 1 depicts the preparation method utilized to synthesize the CoAl-LDH. Advantageously, aluminum is highly soluble in the room temperature liquid EGaIn alloy, resulting in high yield synthesis of aluminum oxide compound on the alloy surface. When the alloy is in contact with water, the dissolved Al in the EGaIn alloy will diffuse to the surface of the liquid metal, and reacts with surrounding water to produce 2D $\mathsf{A l}(\mathsf{O H})_{3}$ (shown with light yellow color in Fig. 1) [28], Figure S2a shows the XRD results, proving its successful preparation. Interestingly, electrostatically polarized $\mathsf{A l}(\mathsf{O H})_{3}$ will adsorb any dissolved metal ions inside the water solution. When dimethylimidazole and $C0^{2+}$ are added to deionized water, it will preferentially combine with $C0^{2+}$ to form ZIF 67 polyhedron, initially resulting in a purple-colored solution. The ionized $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{H}}^{+}$ from deionized water will gradually transform the polyhedron to the stable cubic topological structure of ZIF 67. Dimethylimidazole will combine with $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{H}}^{+}$ and the weakly alkaline environment will eventuate in the formation of CoAl-LDH (indicated with brown color in Fig. 1) [21]. The solution will gradually turn from purple to dark green during the preparation process, proving its formation. In the methanol solution, the cubic structure of ZIF 67 exists in a stable state, then the ordered Co atoms in CoAl-LDH can serve as nucleation and growth sites. At the same time the CoAl-LDH surface undergoes an in-situ coordination reaction, in which the CoMOF crystal has a strong chemical bond, and finally the CoAlLDH@ZIF 67 is prepared (indicated with purple colour in Fig. 1). When CoAl-LDH@ZIF 67 is pyrolyzed in a nitrogen atmosphere, CoAl-LDO@Co-C can be obtained (indicated with green colour in Fig. 1).
The phase structure of the samples was tested using XRD, the results are shown in Fig. 2a and Figure S2. In Figure S2b, the diffraction peak of CoAl-LDH(the brown one in Fig. 1 JCPDS#510045) can be clearly observed, but the relatively less sharp diffraction peak indicating that the crystallinity of CoAlLDH is low [26]. After synthesizing ZIF 67 on CoAl-LDH, the diffraction peaks of ZIF 67 and CoAl-LDH were successfully observed in CoAl-LDH@ZIF67 (Figure S2b). When CoAl-LDH@ZIF 67 is annealed in nitrogen, the organic ligands in ZIF67 will decompose and reduce $C0^{2+}$ to Co elemental substance, and CoAl-LDO@Co-C can be prepared. The XRD pattern of CoAlLDO@Co-C (Fig. 2a) clearly shows that it belongs to the diffraction of Co and graphite C. As shown in Fig. 2a, the diffraction peaks at $44.1^{\circ}\mathrm{~.~}$ , $51.5^{\circ}$ , and $75.8^{\circ}$ belong to the crystal planes (1 1 1), (2 0 0), and (220) of cobalt. Similarly, the (002) diffraction peak at around $26^{\circ}$ belongs to graphitic carbon [12]. The XRD pattern of CoAl-LDH which is directly annealed in nitrogen (CoAl-LDO) is shown in Figure S2c, and the diffraction peak can be observed as a mixture of $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4},$ CoO and Co. It is worth mentioning that the samples in Fig. 2a do not directly show the diffraction peak of Co metal oxide. The reason may be that part of the Co element in CoAl-LDH participates in the nucleation process of ZIF 67, assuming the role of the nucleation site of ZIF 67, whereas some of the Co elements form CoAl-LDO. The XRD patterns of Al $(\mathrm{OH})_{3}@\mathrm{ZIF}$ 67 and $\mathsf{A l}_{2}\mathsf{O}_{3}@\mathsf{C o}-\mathsf{C}$ are shown in Figure S2d, and the diffraction peaks belonging to the elemental substances of ZIF 67 and Co can be clearly observed. Due to the low activity of alumina, its diffraction peaks are not observed.
Raman spectroscopy was used to detect the degree of graphitization and surface structure of materials. As shown in Fig. 2b, two different peaks can be observed at $1340\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ and $1580\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ , corresponding to the D and G bands, respectively. Generally, the D band reflects the structural defects and disorder of carbon which originates from symmetric breathing mode, while the G band is related to graphitic carbon which comes from the vibrations of $\mathsf{s p}^{2}$ carbon. Therefore, the intensity ratio of peak D to peak G $\mathrm{(I_{D}/}$ ${\mathrm{I}}_{\mathrm{G}}{\mathrm{,}}$ can be used to indicate the degree of graphitization [29]. The $\mathrm{I_{D}/I_{G}}$ peak intensity ratio is close to 1.07, which proves that all samples have a similar graphitization degree at the same annealing temperature. Graphitized carbon can provide samples with good electrical conductivity and conduction loss, while amorphous graphite can also be used as a defect-rich region to provide polarization loss [30]. There are some obvious peaks in the range of $465\thinspace{\mathrm{cm}}^{-1}$ , $503\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ and $660~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ , related to the vibration modes of Eg, $\mathbf{F}_{2}\mathbf{g}$ and $\mathsf{A}_{1}\mathsf{g}$ in the spinel structure, respectively [31,32,33]. These vibration modes are derived from the ionic bond formed between Co ions and oxygen in the tetrahedral gap and the octahedral gap in $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4},$ , which proves the existence of CoAl-LDO. The oxygen vacancies in the spinel structure can be used as polarization centers to dissipate the energy of electromagnetic waves [34]. The Raman spectra of CoAl-LDO and $\mathsf{A l}_{2}\mathsf{O}_{3}@\mathsf{C o}-\mathsf{C}$ samples are shown in Figure S3. It can be seen that only the vibration peaks belonging to the spinel structure appear in the CoAl-LDO sample. At the same time, weak peaks related to the D band and the G band are observed. It may be related to the reduction of 2-MI in the layered structure of LDH [24]. Only the D and G bands are observed in $\mathsf{A l}_{2}\mathsf{O}_{3}@\mathsf{C}_{0-}\mathsf{C}$ . In summary, the successful preparation of the CoAl$\scriptstyle{\mathrm{LDO@Co-C}}$ heterojunction structure can be proved. The existence of a heterojunction structure can bring a rich interface and provide interface polarization, which is conducive to the absorption of electromagnetic waves.
The information on the chemical structure of the samples was tested via FT-IR spectra, as shown in Fig. 2c. The peak around $500\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ represents the chemical bond between metal and oxygen (Al-O Co-O), which proves the existence of CoAl-LDO, and interestingly, the samples exhibited some identical carbon related peaks, $c{-}0$ (about $1040\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ ), and $\scriptstyle{\mathsf{C}}=0$ (about $1630{\mathrm{cm}}^{-1}$ ) [35]. Moreover, there were some organic functional groups, including $0{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{H}$ (at around $3440\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ ) and $C{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{H}$ (at around $2921~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ ) [15]. The FTIR results are highly consistent with XRD and Raman, which proves that CoAl-LDO@Co-C has been successfully synthesized. The functional groups remaining in the sample can be used as the polarization center to form an electric dipole, which can promote the loss of electromagnetic waves to a certain extent.
The specific surface area and pore size distribution of the samples are shown in Fig. 2d-i. It can clearly be observed that all samples exhibit the type-Ⅳ isotherm of adsorption characteristics. The hysteresis loop can be seen when the relative pressure $\scriptstyle({\mathsf{p}}/{\mathsf{p0}})$ is in the range of $0.6\substack{-1.0}$ . The pore size distribution of the sample is shown as an inset. It can be seen that the pore size of the sample is mostly distributed in $2{-}4\ \mathrm{nm}$ and $7-12{\mathrm{nm}}$ , reflecting the characteristics of mesopores. The mesopores in the sample may be caused by the lack of organic ligands in the ZIF 67 polyhedron during the pyrolysis process and the escape of trace gases. The mesopores in the samples may be caused by the lack of organic ligands in the ZIF 67 polyhedron during the pyrolysis process and the escape of trace gases. At the same time, the specific surface areas of the samples were $48.97\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}$ for CoAl-LDO@Co-C-1, $70.39\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{~}$ g for CoAl-LDO@Co-C-2, $114.12\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}$ for CoAl-LDO@Co-C-3, respectively. It can be seen that the CoAl-LDO@Co-C-3 sample has a larger specific surface area, which proves that the honeycomb structure can increase the specific surface area to a certain extent. The larger specific surface area has a good promotion effect on the scattering and dissipation of electromagnetic waves and can improve the electromagnetic wave absorption performance.
The morphology of the samples was determined using SEM, Fig. 3a shows two-dimensional $\mathsf{A l}(\mathsf{O H})_{3}$ , showing 2D layered morphology. The CoAl-LDH synthesized with dimethylimidazole, shown in Fig. 3b, retains the two-dimensional lamellar structure as a whole, and the Al atoms provided by $\mathsf{A l}(\mathsf{O H})_{3}$ can adsorb cobalt ion in the solution to form CoAl-LDH. Figure S4 a and b show the morphology of CoAl-LDO, it can be clearly seen that the twodimensional lamellar structure can be retained after annealing. Figure S4c-f shows the morphology of ZIF67 directly grown on Al $(\mathrm{OH})_{3}$ and its derivative $\mathsf{A l}_{2}\mathsf{O}_{3}@\mathsf{C}_{0-}\mathsf{C}$ . It is shown that ZIF 67 achieved polyhedrons surface morphologies, which after annealing, are transformed into collapsed polyhedron honeycomb-like nano-morphologies.
Fig. 3 e, i and m show the microstructures of the precursors of CoAl-LDH $@$ ZIF 67-1, CoAl-LDH@ZIF 67-2 and CoAl-LDH@ZIF 67-3, respectively. When the content of $C0^{2+}$ and methylimidazole precursors are kept unchanged, and the amount of CoAl-LDH are gradually reduced, the morphology of the prepared CoAl-LDH $@$ ZIF67 morphology will be substantially influenced. When the content of CoAl-LDH is higher, the relative concentration of $C o^{2+}$ and 2- MI is low, so the higher concentration of CoAl-LDH in methanol will provide more sites for the nucleation of ZIF 67, thereby limiting the further growth of ZIF67 grains [36]. Therefore, as shown in Fig. 3e, the ZIF 67 grains in the CoAl-LDH@ZIF-1 have a relatively small average diameter. When the content of CoAl-LDH gradually decreases, the size of the ZIF 67 in CoAl-LDH@ZIF-2 polyhedron increases, but it does not achieve a complete and uniform coating effect on the CoAl-LDH surface. As the content of CoAl-LDH further decreases, the size of the ZIF 67 polyhedron has not changed significantly, but it forms a uniform coating effect on the CoAl-LDH surface. It can be seen that the arrangement of ZIF 67 is denser and more uniform than CoAl-LDH@ZIF-2.
The SEM morphologies of the samples after annealing in nitrogen are shown in Fig. 3 g, k and o. CoAl-LDO@Co-C-1 is found to retain the original morphology after annealing, and the ZIF 67 particles are attached to CoAl-LDH. In CoAl-LDO@Co-C-2, it can be seen that the ZIF 67 polyhedron is embedded in the surface of CoAl-LDH. However, due to the insufficient coating amount of ZIF 67, a larger area of contact cannot be formed between the polyhedrons, so it cannot be formed into a honeycomb structure after annealing. Interestingly, the presence of rod-shaped carbon nanotubes was observed on the surface of ZIF 67. The reason for its formation may be the small movement of Co during the annealing process, which caused the carbon in the organic ligand to be reduced to carbon nanotubes. On the contrary, in CoAl-LDO@CoC-3, the ZIF67 particles that were originally uniformly coated have collapsed, the polyhedron morphology disappears, and the collapsed ZIF 67 is attached to the CoAl-LDO surface, replacing the ‘‘honeycomb” structure. Figure S5 shows the element distribution of CoAl-LDO@Co-C-3, and it can be seen that the element distribution of the prepared sample is uniform. Zhang et al. provided an explanation for the formation of this morphology [37]: before the annealing treatment, the ZIF67 particles are uniformly attached together, and the high-temperature transformation process causes the polyhedron to collapse from the inside, but the edges of the polyhedron are retained, and then form a ‘‘honeycomb-like” nano-morphology. It is worth mentioning that the honeycomb morphologies are regarded as an ideal electromagnetic wave absorbing material model, and its larger specific surface area and channel can increase the path of electromagnetic wave loss, thereby improving its performance [38,39,40].
Fig. 4 shows the TEM images of CoAl-LDO@Co-C-3. From Fig. 4a, b and c we could observe clearly that ZIF 67 derivatives are uniformly attached to the surface of CoAl-LDO. From Fig. 4d-f we can see that Co particles are embedded in the carbon and CoAl LDO matrix. In Fig. $4{\mathrm{g}}$ and h, the images of HRTEM show that the spacing lattice of $0.34\mathrm{nm}$ and $0.206\mathrm{nm}$ corresponds to the (002) and (1 1 1) lattice planes of C and Co particles, respectively [41]. Cobalt is uniformly coated by graphitized carbon, which can become the center of electromagnetic wave scattering and electromagnetic wave loss. As shown in Fig. 4i, selected areas electron diffraction (SAED) was carried out, showing the (111) (20 0) and (220) crystal planes of cobalt (JCPDS#150806) and (002) crystal planes of graphitized carbon (JCPDS#411487). EDX maps in Fig. 4j indicate the distribution of various elements to be dispersed throughout on the scanned flake (including elements Al, C, N, O and Co). The characterization results are consistent with XRD and Raman, proving the successful synthesis of CoAl-LDO@Co-C.
X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) was utilized to detect the valence and state of the chemical elements in the sample. The results are shown in Fig. 5 and Figure S6. Figure S6a shows the high-resolution spectrum of Al, the peak at $74.8\:\mathrm{eV}$ proves the existence of ${\sf A l}_{2}{\sf O}_{3}$ . Fig. 5a and Figure S6b show the XPS survey spectrum, in which Al, Co, C, O and N can be clearly seen, which proves the successful synthesis of the samples. The N1s spectrum (Fig. 5b) can be deconvoluted into four characteristic peaks, at 398.7 eV, 399.7 eV, $400.9\mathrm{eV}$ and $404.0\mathrm{eV}$ , corresponding to pyridine, pyrrole, graphite N and oxide N, respectively [42]. It is understood that the graphitic $\mathsf{N}$ and pyridinic N are suitable for conduction loss, and pyrrolic N is preferred for the polarization losses, all of which increase dielectric loss and microwave absorption ability [41]. In the high-resolution spectrum of carbon (Fig. 5c), the peaks at $284.3\mathrm{eV}$ , $286.1\ \mathrm{eV}$ and $289.7\mathrm{eV}$ can be assigned to ${\mathsf{C}}{\mathrm{-}}{\mathsf{C}}/{\mathsf{C}}{\mathrm{=}}{\mathsf{C}}$ , CAO and $\scriptstyle{\mathsf{C}}=0$ , respectively, which is consistent with the FTIR results. The high-resolution spectrum of cobalt is shown in Fig. 5d, there are two spin-orbit split double peaks, corresponding to $C0^{2+}$ $782.9\mathrm{eV}$ and $797.6\mathrm{eV})$ and ${\mathsf{C o}}^{3+}$ $(780.6\mathrm{eV}$ and $796.3\mathrm{eV},$ . The two peaks, located at $778.1\ \mathrm{eV}$ and $793.7\mathrm{eV}$ , corresponding to ${\mathsf{C o}}^{0}$ . The high-resolution spectrum of cobalt element shows that there are multiple valence states of cobalt in the sample, including elemental and oxidation states. Figure S6c shows the high-resolution spectrum of Co element in CoAlLDO. In contrast to $\mathsf{C o A l-L D O@C o-C}$ , the peak corresponding to zero-valent cobalt is significantly reduced, which proves that only a small amount of metallic Co is present in CoAl-LDO, this is consistent with the XRD result. At the same time, it is proved that the introduction of ZIF 67 can provide a large amount of reduced metal cobalt element and provide a magnetic source for the samples. The XPS detection results are consistent with the EDX mapping and previous detection results, which proves the successful synthesis of CoAl-LDO@Co-C.
The complex permittivity $\left(\varepsilon_{\mathrm{{r}}}=\varepsilon^{\prime}\mathrm{~\boldmath~-~}\mathrm{j}\varepsilon^{\prime\prime}\right)$ and permeability $(\upmu_{\mathrm{r}}=\upmu^{\prime}-\mathrm{j}\upmu^{\prime\prime})$ of the samples are tested using a Vector Network Analyzer (VNA). The real part of the permittivity and the real part of the permeability represents the storage capacity of the electromagnetic field, and the imaginary part represents the loss capacity of the electromagnetic field [43]. Then, tan ${\delta_{\mathrm{{m}}}}={\upmu^{\ast}}/{\upmu^{\prime}}$ and tan $\delta_{\mathrm{e}}=\varepsilon^{\prime\prime}/\varepsilon^{\prime}$ are defined as magnetic loss tangent and dielectric loss tangent respectively.
Fig. 6 and Figure S7a show the electromagnetic parameters of permittivity in all samples, where the real and imaginary parts of the permittivity are characterized by a downward trend with the increase of frequency. This is in line with the change law of the dielectric spectrum, which indicates that the polarization process of the dielectric cannot keep up with the change of the electromagnetic field and thus has a hysteresis [3]. The variation ranges of the real part of the permittivity of CoAl-LDO@Co-C-3, CoAl-LDO@Co-C2 and CoAl-LDO@Co-C-1 are 9.5–4.2, 8.9–4.8, and 7.4–5.5 respectively. It can be seen that as the content of CoAl-LDH decreases, the dielectric properties of the sample are further enhanced for microwave absorption. This may be attributed to the fact that CoAl-LDO has low conductivity $(\sigma_{0}=\omega\varepsilon_{0}\varepsilon_{r}^{\prime\prime}=2\pi f\varepsilon_{0}\varepsilon_{r}^{\prime\prime})$ [15]. The CoAl-LDO in Figure S7a has relatively low real and imaginary parts of permittivity, which confirms our statement. It can be seen that as the content of CoAl-LDO decreases, the dielectric properties of the sample are further improved by reduction of the real part of the permittivity, When the conductivity of the sample increases, more bound charges are induced on the contact surface of the medium to store the energy of the electromagnetic field. At the same time, the honeycomb sample CoAl-LDO@Co-C-3 can provide more heterojunction interfaces and form more space charges to continue to enhance the storage capacity of electromagnetic waves.
The imaginary part of the permittivity of CoAl-LDO@Co-C-3 and CoAl-LDO@Co-C-2 is in a higher range compared to CoAl-LDO@CoC-1. Considering that they have relatively close $\mathrm{I_{D}/I_{G}}$ values (concerning Raman spectroscopy), it can be considered that the honeycomb-like structure brings better conduction loss, and the construction of the internal conductive network is more complete. Some minor peaks appear in the test frequency range of the three samples, related to the Debye relaxation process, which indicate that there is a polarization relaxation process inside the sample [23]. Fig. 6c indicates that the CoAl-LDO@Co-C-3 sample has the largest dielectric loss tangent value within the test frequency range, indicating that it has the best electromagnetic wave absorption performance
Under normal circumstances, the dielectric loss of electromagnetic waves in the absorbing material can be divided into conduction and polarization loss. The conduction loss is related to the construction of the conductive path in the sample. When the electromagnetic wave is absorbed by the material, the incident electromagnetic wave will form a microcurrent in the conductive network inside the material. The form dissipates, and then the electromagnetic energy is converted into heat energy. The polarization loss is the energy loss due to the rotation of the electric dipole moment generated by the bound charge in the dielectric under the alternating electromagnetic field [44]. The polarization loss relies on rotation between adjacent dipoles to generate energy loss. Therefore, we use the Debye relaxation model to better describe the complex permittivity:
$$
\varepsilon_{\mathrm{{r}}}=\varepsilon_{\infty}+{\frac{\varepsilon_{s}-\varepsilon_{\infty}}{1+j2\pi f\tau}}=\varepsilon^{\prime}-j\varepsilon^{\prime\prime}
$$
$$
\varepsilon^{\prime}=\varepsilon_{\infty}+\frac{\varepsilon_{s}-\varepsilon_{\infty}}{1+j\left(2\pi f\right)^{2}\tau^{2}}
$$
$$
\varepsilon^{\prime\prime}=\frac{2\pi f\tau(\varepsilon_{s}-\varepsilon_{\infty})}{1+\left(2\pi f\right)^{2}\tau^{2}}
$$
After considering the effect of conduction loss, the Debye formula can be expressed as:
$$
\varepsilon^{\prime\prime}=\varepsilon_{\mathrm{p}}^{\prime\prime}+\varepsilon_{\mathrm{c}}^{\prime\prime}={\left(\varepsilon_{s}-\varepsilon_{\infty}\right)}\frac{2\pi f\tau}{1+{\left(2\pi f\right)}^{2}\tau^{2}}+\frac{\sigma}{2\pi f\varepsilon_{0}}
$$
where $\scriptstyle{\varepsilon_{\infty}}$ is the light frequency permittivity, $\varepsilon_{s}$ is the static permittivity, $\boldsymbol{\tau}$ is the relaxation time and f is the frequency [14]. By fitting the imaginary part of the dielectric constant using the least square method, the conduction and polarization loss are shown in Fig. 6d and Fig. 6e, respectively. It can be seen that the loss type of the three samples at low frequency is mainly provided by conduction loss, while the loss type at high frequency is dominated by polarization loss. CoAl-LDO@Co-C-3 has good polarization loss at low frequencies, which may be due to a large number of heterojunction interfaces between the LDO graphitized carbon and metallic cobalt, which in turn forms a large amount of space charge accumulation, thereby providing superior polarization loss, and the CoAl-LDO@CoC-3 has relatively higher conduction and polarization loss in the entire frequency range. This is due to the metallic cobalt and graphitized carbon in the ZIF 67 derivative provide good conductivity for the sample, which enhances the conduction loss. It can be seen that, overall, the conduction loss and polarization loss of the three samples have gradually increased, which shows that through reasonable control of the microstructure, the honeycomb-like absorbing structure obtained can obtain good dielectric loss.
According to the Debye relaxation theory, the expression of the Cole-Cole circle can be obtained after rewriting formula (1) (2) (3):
$$
\left(\varepsilon^{\prime}-\frac{\varepsilon_{s}+\varepsilon_{\infty}}{2}\right)+\left(\varepsilon^{\prime\prime}\right)^{2}=\left(\frac{\varepsilon_{s}-\varepsilon_{\infty}}{2}\right)^{2}
$$
When the Debye relaxation process occurs in the sample, the corresponding semicircle can be found in the corresponding frequency range in the Cole-Cole curve [45]. Fig. 6f, $\mathbf{g}$ and h show the Cole-Cole curve circle of the three samples, which can be observed in the test range of $2{-}18\operatorname{GHz}$ . There are multiple Debye relaxation processes in the inside. When the Debye relaxation process occurs, the adjacent electric dipoles inside the samples rotate in a small range, and the energy of the electromagnetic wave is consumed in this process. There are several obvious Cole-Cole semicircles in CoAl-LDO@Co-C-1, indicating that the polarization loss occupies the main position, but combined with the analysis of the dielectric imaginary part, the loss provided by the polarization process is relatively small. The Cole-Cole curve in lowfrequency bands of CoAl-LDO@Co-C-2 and CoAl-LDO@Co-C-3 gradually become a slanted straight line, indicating that the transmission loss is the main loss method, which is consistent with the previous analysis, and Cole-Cole semicircles also appeared at high frequencies. At the heterojunction interface, there are large numbers of heterogeneous charges at both the nitrogen-doped and amorphous graphite, which can be used as polarization centers, which in turn causes polarization loss [46].
The permeability of the samples are shown in Fig. 7 and Figure S7b. The initial permeability of CoAl-LDO@Co-C-3, CoAlLDO@Co-C-2 and CoAl-LDO@Co-C-1 are 1.43, 1.32, and 1.21 (Fig. 7a), respectively. It can be seen that the CoAl-LDO@Co-C-3 with a honeycomb structure has a higher imaginary part of complex permeability (Fig. 7b), indicating that it has a better magnetic loss capability. Fig. 7c shows the magnetic loss tangent of the samples, which can preliminarily judge the loss capability. It can be seen that in the frequency range of $2{\cdot}18\operatorname{GHz}$ , the CoAl$\mathsf{L D O@C o-C-3}$ sample has a higher magnetic loss tangent, indicating that it has excellent magnetic loss properties and can produce excellent impedance matching, which subsequently results in an exccelent electromagnetic wave absorption effect.
The saturation magnetization and coercivity of the samples were tested with a vibrating sample magnetometer. The test results are shown in Fig. 7d. The saturation magnetization of the three samples are $93.06\mathrm{{emu}/g,67.11\mathrm{{emu}/g\ 58.78\mathrm{{emu}/g}}}$ respectively, and the coercive forces are 190.81, 70.52 and 24.62, respectively. Therefore, it can be observed that $\mathsf{L D O@C o-C-3}$ has the highest saturation magnetization. The reason that the sample has a higher permeability may be due to a higher saturation magnetization [41]. The types of magnetic loss can be divided into eddy current loss, hysteresis loss, domain wall resonance, exchange resonance and natural resonance. Domain wall resonance and exchange resonance usually occur in the low-frequency range and high-frequency range respectively, where the domain wall resonance is generally not considered in the frequency range of the test. Therefore, it can be considered that natural resonance and eddy current loss are the main sources of magnetic loss in the samples [35]. It is generally believed that the resonance peak of the permeability of magnetic materials in the low-frequency range is mostly related to natural resonance, and when the alternating electromagnetic field passes through the interior of the absorbing material, according to the Maxwell equation, the corresponding induced current will also be generated in the interior to prevent its change trend. The relational formula describing the eddy current loss is:
$$
C_{0}=\mu^{\prime\prime}(\mu^{\prime})^{-2}f^{-1}=2\pi\mu_{0}d^{2}\sigma
$$
when eddy current loss dominates, the value of ${\sf C}_{0}$ will become a straight line [47]. Fig. 7e shows the ${\sf C}_{0}$ value of the sample. It can be seen that the ${\sf C}_{0}$ curve of the sample shows a downward trend in the entire test frequency range, indicating that the eddy current loss does not occupy a dominant position, and the main source of magnetic loss can be attributed to the natural resonance.
The electromagnetic wave absorption performance of the sample is evaluated by the reflection loss value. According to the transmission line theory, the reflection loss value is:
$$
R L(d B)=20\mathrm{log}\bigg|\frac{Z_{i n}-1}{Z_{i n}+1}\bigg|
$$
$$
Z_{i n}={\sqrt{\frac{\mu_{r}}{\varepsilon_{r}}}}\mathrm{tanh}\left[j{\frac{2\pi f d}{c}}{\sqrt{\varepsilon_{r}\mu_{r}}}\right]
$$
$Z_{\mathrm{in}}$ is the normalized input impedance, f is the frequency of electromagnetic waves, d is the thickness of the tested sample, and c is the speed of light. The reflection loss can measure the strength of electromagnetic wave absorption. When the reflection loss value is $-10\mathrm{dB}$ , it indicates that $90\%$ of the electromagnetic wave has been effectively absorbed, and the effective absorption bandwidth (EAB) indicates that the reflection loss value is less than the frequency range of $-10{\mathrm{dB}}$ under a specific thickness [48]. Fig. 8 and Figure S8 depict the reflection loss of the three samples. As can be seen in Figure S8, CoAl-LDO has poor electromagnetic wave absorption performance, and there is no EAB in the test frequency band. When the ZIF 67 derivative is loaded, the minimum reflection loss can reach $-37.1$ dB, but the higher thickness $7.5\mathrm{mm}$ $3.34\mathrm{GHz}^{\cdot}$ ) limits its further application. The reflection loss value of the CofAl-LDO@Co-C-1 sample is relatively lower, and the effective absorption bandwidth is $3.86\mathsf{G H z}$ under $2.5\mathrm{mm}$ . When the content of CoAl-LDO is reduced, the reflection loss of the CoAl$\mathsf{L D O@C o-C-2}$ is improved, the minimum reflection loss reaches 51.66 dB, and the effective absorption bandwidth is $4.40\mathsf{G H z}$ at $3.0\mathrm{mm}$ . Although the minimum reflection loss value of CoAl$\mathsf{L D O@C o-C-3}$ has been reduced to 38.18 dB, the effective absorption bandwidth is further increased. The absorption bandwidth reached $8.48\:{\mathrm{GHz}}$ at $2.6\mathrm{mm}$ , showing excellent efficient electromagnetic wave absorption and confirming that the CoAl$\mathsf{L D O@C o-C-3}$ sample demonstrated the best electromagnetic wave absorption effect.
Suitable impedance matching is paramount to achieving effective electromagnetic wave propagation into the material. Appropriate dielectric and permeability play an important role in impedance matching. Fig. 9a and b show the schematic diagram of the input impedance of the samples at different thicknesses. According to transmission line theory, the closer the value of the normalized input impedance $Z_{\mathrm{in}}$ is to 1, the smaller the proportion of reflected electromagnetic waves, and the more electromagnetic waves can enter the material. It can be seen that the value of $Z_{\mathrm{in}}$ in CoAl-LDO@Co-C-2 and CoAl-LDO@Co-C-3 are close to 1 $\left(Z_{\mathrm{in}}-1=0\right)$ at different thicknesses, it can achieve good impedance matching at different thicknesses so that electromagnetic waves can enter the interior of the absorbing material to the greatest extent [49].
When electromagnetic waves propagate into the bulk of the absorbing material, the electromagnetic attenuation characteristics are determined by the attenuation coefficient [50], which is usually described as:
$$
\alpha{=}\frac{\sqrt{2}\pi f}{c}\times\sqrt{\left(\mu^{'}\varepsilon^{'}-\mu^{'}\varepsilon^{'}\right)+\left(\mu^{'}\varepsilon^{'}-\mu^{'}\varepsilon^{'}\right)^{2}+\left(\mu^{'}\varepsilon^{'}+\mu^{'}\varepsilon^{'}\right)^{2}}
$$
As shown in Fig. 9c, the attenuation coefficients of the samples are arranged in the order of $\mathrm{CoAl\mathrm{-}L D O@C o\mathrm{-}C\mathrm{-}3>C o A l\mathrm{-}L D O@C o\mathrm{-}C\mathrm{-}}$ $2\succ\mathrm{CoAl\mathrm{-}L D O@C o\mathrm{-}C\mathrm{-}1>A l_{2}O_{3}@C o\mathrm{-}C\succeq C o A l\mathrm{-}L D O}$ . The $\textsf{\textsf{q}}$ (attenuation constant in equation (9)) values of CoAl-LDO@Co-C-3 and CoAl-LDO@Co-C-2 samples are relatively large, which can explain their excellent electromagnetic wave absorption performances. Table 1 shows the comparison results of different electromagnetic wave absorbing materials, and it can be seen that the CoAl$\mathsf{L D O@C o-C-2}$ and CoAl-LDO@Co-C-2 prepared in this work have wider effective absorption bandwidth with thinner matching thickness.
Furthermore, the principle of electromagnetic wave absorption can also be explained by using the quarter-wavelength theory [2]. Note that the frequency corresponding to the minimum value of the reflection loss is shifted to a high-frequency with an increase in the thickness, which can be explained by the following formula00:
$$
\mathbf{\Deltat}_{m}=n\lambda/4=n c/(4f_{m}\sqrt{|\mu_{r}||\varepsilon_{r}|})(\mathbf{n}=1,3,5\cdots)
$$
where $\mathbf{t}_{\mathrm{m}}$ is the matching thickness, $\lambda$ is the wavelength, c is the speed of light, and $\mathbf{f}_{\mathrm{m}}$ is the matching frequency. When the thickness of the absorbing material is equal to an odd multiple of a quarter wavelength, since the phase difference between the incident electromagnetic wave and the reflected electromagnetic wave is half a period, the amplitude of the electromagnetic wave in the two directions can be eliminated. Fig. 9 a and b compare the simulations against the calculations, where it can be seen that they are both in good agreement.
In summary, the possible mechanism of electromagnetic wave absorption and loss is shown in Fig. 10. First of all, the excellent electromagnetic parameters provided by CoAl-LDO, carbon and magnetic particles can create good impedance matching conditions and allow electromagnetic waves to enter the material to the greatest extent. Secondly, a large number of heterojunction interfaces and graphitized carbon in CoAl-LDO@Co-C provide good polarization and conduction loss, so that electromagnetic waves can be dissipated inside the material. At the same time, the uniformly dispersed cobalt element can cause magnetic loss and further promote electromagnetic wave absorption performance.
## 4. Conclusion
In summary, we innovatively use room temperature liquid metals as a reaction platform to prepare a library of two-dimensional layered double oxide compounds as a template to prepare twodimensional nanoarrays of CoAl-LDH. The room temperature liquid metal synthesis method can rapidly produce high yields of twodimensional $\mathsf{A l}(\mathsf{O H})_{3}$ with large lateral dimensions through low toxicity and sustainable route. We use the excellent chemical compatibility of CoAl-LDH and ZIF 67 to prepare CoAl-LDH@ ZIF 67 and its derivatives. By adjusting the content of CoAl-LDH, the honeycomb nano-morphology electromagnetic wave absorbing material can be efficiently tuned. It is demonstrated that CoAl-LDO@Co-C-3 has an effective absorption bandwidth of $8.48\:{\mathrm{GHz}}$ at $2.6\mathrm{mm}$ . The high electromagnetic wave absorption effect comes from the exceptional magnetoelectric synergy effect in the sample. This work not only explores a simple and efficient synthesis method for the preparation of Al-based LDH, but also provides a new idea for the preparation of carbon-based heterojunction structures. The prepared CoAl-LDO@Co-C may be widely used in energy storage and catalysis and may have broad application prospects in the future.
